# Tell GitHub's Linguist to ignore vendored Go dependencies when computing
# language stats, file-tree summaries, and "Files changed" PR diffs.
vendor/** linguist-vendored
tools/vendor/** linguist-vendored

# Route the backlog through the custom merge driver, which resolves Queue-table
# conflicts by row ID instead of by line position (adjacent adds and deletes are
# what a plain three-way merge cannot absorb, and the process concentrates them
# at the top of the table). Git deliberately does not let a tracked file define
# the driver's command, so the `merge.backlog.driver` config is per-clone and
# opt-in: `make merge-driver`. Without it, git falls back to its built-in
# three-way merge and this line is a no-op.
docs/STATUS.md merge=backlog

# Same treatment for the script registry, keyed on the script path in column 1.
# check-script-docs.sh requires a row for every gated script, so a PR adding one
# has no choice but to edit this file, and two such PRs land rows in the same
# group table. Per-clone and opt-in via `make merge-driver`, for the reason
# below; without it git falls back to its built-in three-way merge.
scripts/README.md merge=scriptindex

# And the gate and suite lists, merged entry by entry. They live in their own
# file rather than in the Makefile precisely so this line can exist: routing is
# per file, and routing the whole Makefile would make every ordinary change to
# it count as driver-owned downstream. Per-clone and opt-in, same as the rest.
mk/gate-lists.mk merge=gatelists

# Same treatment for the plan index, keyed on the plan path in column 1 rather
# than a backlog row ID: every plan that lands adds one long row near the same
# neighbours, so concurrent branches collide by construction. Per-clone and
# opt-in for the same reason, via the same `make merge-driver`; without it git
# falls back to its built-in three-way merge and this line is a no-op.
docs/plan/README.md merge=planindex

# And the public roadmap, keyed on each bullet's `<!-- q:QN -->` backlog
# annotation. Shipping a gated item deletes its bullet, so two gate PRs in the
# same window delete adjacent bullets and conflict by construction. Per-clone
# and opt-in for the same reason, via the same `make merge-driver`; without it
# git falls back to its built-in three-way merge and this line is a no-op.
docs/roadmap.md merge=roadmap
